# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Portfolio — what does not belong in history
#
# Rule of thumb: anything the build can regenerate, anything the app writes at
# runtime, and anything that is yours rather than the project's.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---- .NET build output ----------------------------------------------------
bin/
obj/
artifacts/
publish/
TestResults/
*.user
*.suo
.vs/
.idea/
.vscode/

# The SDK and the NuGet cache live in ~/.local (see env.sh) so they stay out of
# the repo and out of snapshots. These are here only for the case where someone
# points DOTNET_ROOT or NUGET_PACKAGES inside the checkout instead.
.dotnet/
.nuget/

# ---- client ---------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
*.tsbuildinfo
.vite/

# The UI smoke bundle (esbuild output, ~1.3 MB) - built by `npm run test:ui`.
src/Client/dist/

# The built client. `npm run build` in src/Client emits it here so one process
# serves both the API and the UI, which makes it generated output: a fresh clone
# runs `npm install && npm run build` (or `npm run dev` for hot reload) and the
# directory reappears. Committing it would put a 200 kB minified bundle in every
# diff. If you would rather ship it, force it in deliberately:
#   git add -f src/Server/wwwroot
src/Server/wwwroot/

# ---- runtime state --------------------------------------------------------
# Your portfolio, the one automatic .bak kept beside it, and the on-disk cache
# of provider responses. All three are created on first run; the app starts
# empty without them and both live test suites seed their own state, so nothing
# here is needed to build or test.
#
# This is also the file that holds what you own, so it should never end up in
# history by accident. To ship the demo portfolio on purpose:
#   git add -f src/Server/data/portfolio.json
src/Server/data/

# Secrets and machine-local config. env.sh is NOT ignored: it only puts the SDK
# on PATH and holds no credentials, and the app needs no API keys at all.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pfx
secrets.json
appsettings.*.Local.json
deploy.conf

# ---- logs -----------------------------------------------------------------
*.log
logs/

# ---- python (tests/api_e2e.py) -------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.venv/
venv/

# ---- editors and operating systems ---------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*~
*.swp
*.orig
*.rej
